(Regression test: buffer equality comparison used _str_eq which
NUL-scans both sides. After clear+shorter-rewrite, the buffer's data
area is not NUL-terminated at the logical end, so _str_eq would walk
into stale bytes and falsely report not-equal when two buffers with
identical logical content were compared.)

Create a buffer called buf1.
Set byte 1 of buf1 to 'a'.
Set byte 2 of buf1 to 'b'.

(buf2: written with 'abcd', cleared, rewritten as 'ab' -
 stale 'cd' remain in the data area beyond logical length 2)
Create a buffer called buf2.
Set byte 1 of buf2 to 'a'.
Set byte 2 of buf2 to 'b'.
Set byte 3 of buf2 to 'c'.
Set byte 4 of buf2 to 'd'.
clear buf2.
Set byte 1 of buf2 to 'a'.
Set byte 2 of buf2 to 'b'.

(Both logically hold 'ab' - must be equal)
If buf1 is buf2 then,
    Print "equal".
If buf1 is not buf2 then,
    Print "BUG: not equal".

(Genuinely different buffers must remain not-equal)
Create a buffer called buf3.
Set byte 1 of buf3 to 'x'.

If buf1 is buf3 then,
    Print "BUG: equal".
If buf1 is not buf3 then,
    Print "not equal".
